# Example 37: Two Services on One Box Behind One Proxy. (co-09, co-11)
#
# The reverse proxy's superpower: one box, one :443, MANY services, each on its
# own subdomain. This Caddyfile routes api.example.com -> app A and
# blog.example.com -> app B, each forwarding to a different local port. One
# proxy, one cert set, two distinct services. Save as /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.

api.example.com {  # => co-11: a distinct subdomain for service A
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000  # => service A (the app from Example 6)
}

blog.example.com {  # => co-11: a distinct subdomain for service B
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001  # => service B (a second app on a second local port)
}

# --- Why this works (co-09) --------------------------------------------------
# The proxy reads the TLS SNI / Host header on each request and routes by NAME.
# Both services share :443 from the caller's view but are isolated behind it --
# the proxy is the single public face that multiplexes them.
# --- Prerequisites -----------------------------------------------------------
#   - DNS A records for BOTH api.example.com and blog.example.com -> the box (co-11)
#   - both apps running on their local ports (8000 and 8001)
# --- Verify ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -s https://api.example.com/health   # => service A answers
#   curl -s https://blog.example.com/        # => service B answers
# [verify] two services, one box, one :443 -- both reachable by name (co-09).